<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">2 June in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 2 June, from 455 to 1953.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/carthage" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carthage.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">455 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,571 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Vandals begin a two-week sack of Rome</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">King Gaiseric's Vandals entered Rome and plundered it for fourteen days, carrying off treasure and captives to Carthage. The episode later gave the word 'vandalism' to European languages.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1098 CE <span class="tih-ago">928 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Crusaders are let into Antioch by night</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">On the night of 2 to 3 June, a tower commander named Firouz let Bohemond's men over the walls, ending a seven-month siege. The crusaders were themselves besieged in the city days later.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1763 CE <span class="tih-ago">263 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Ojibwe warriors seize Fort Michilimackinac</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">During a game of baaga'adowe, a form of lacrosse, Ojibwe players chased the ball into the fort and took it by surprise. The attack was part of the wider uprising known as Pontiac's War.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1896 CE <span class="tih-ago">130 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Guglielmo Marconi applies for a wireless telegraphy patent</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The young Italian inventor filed his application in London for a system of telegraphy without wires. Within five years his signals had crossed the Atlantic.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1924 CE <span class="tih-ago">102 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Coolidge signs the Indian Citizenship Act</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The law granted US citizenship to all Native Americans born in the United States. Some states still kept Native people from voting for decades afterwards.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1953 CE <span class="tih-ago">73 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Elizabeth II is crowned at Westminster Abbey</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The coronation was the first to be televised in full; some 27 million people in Britain watched, many on newly bought sets. It was a landmark in the spread of television.</p></div></li></ol>
